            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for kindly, then the Latin for goodwill."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: cōmis, cōme",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cōmis, cōme — \"courteous, friendly\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Hospes cōmis omnēs benignē salūtat. The courteous host greets everyone kindly."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two endings. Warm, easy, approachable manners."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cōmis comes from Old Latin cosmis (EtymDB), which EtymDB traces to a Proto-Indo-European root meaning \"to smile\"; Lewis & Short calls it doubtful, so the origin is not fully settled."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
